  "metadata": {
    "title": "Infraestrutura Internacional de Pesquisa Redox: Documento Técnico-Programático do Framework Unificado de Transferência de Elétrons para Apresentação à Organização Mundial da Saúde e ao BRICS",
    "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.22738327",
    "publication_date": "2026-09-14",
    "description": "<p>Redox research is fragmented across domains that share a single physical phenomenon -<br>electron transfer - yet measure, store and model their results in incompatible systems. This<br>document consolidates, in a form suitable for submission to the World Health Organization<br>(WHO) and to BRICS health cooperation mechanisms, the Unified Electron Transfer<br>Framework: a proposal for an international research infrastructure organized in four<br>interdependent layers (standardized measurement, interoperable data, multiscale modelling,<br>and design/engineering), with a minimal viable ontology in OWL 2 RL validated by<br>SHACL, extensions to the SBPAX and EMMO ontologies, and FAIR principles. The<br>document specifies a multinational governance model - with WHO providing normative<br>coordination (2022 Data Sharing Policy, CIOMS 2016 guidelines, GCLP and the ICTRP<br>platform) and BRICS providing political legitimacy and financing (New Development<br>Bank, STI Framework Programme, sectoral networks) - as well as a three-phase<br>Infraestrutura Internacional de Pesquisa Redox &mdash; BRICS-OMS<br>P&aacute;gina 3<br>implementation plan (foundation, 0-18 months; multinational clinical pilot, 18-36 months;<br>expansion, 36-60 months), an indicative budget of US$ 10.05 million, phase-specific<br>success indicators and a risk register. It concludes that the required technical infrastructure<br>already exists and is mature; the bottleneck is coordination and adoption, and the greatest<br>risks are political and financial rather than technical. The proposed next step is to convene<br>the founding consortium, sign the charter and launch Phase 1.</p>",
